{-
   The rules a generic parameter's default has to satisfy - Implementation-Const-Generics.md §1.8.

   Each is checked where the default is *written*, which is the point: a default is part of the
   declaration's API, so a program that reads the head can tell what an omitted argument gets. A
   rule checked at the use site instead would report a mistake in a declaration against whoever
   applied it.
-}

data Wrapper(a) {value: a}

class Hidden(a):
  fn conceal(value: a) -> Int

-- A default that names another parameter of the same list. What `b` meant would depend on the order
-- the arguments were filled in, and there is no reading of that worth having.
data Chained(a, b = a) {left: a, right: b}

-- The two kinds crossed: a const parameter takes a number, a type parameter takes a type, and which
-- is which is what the annotation says. `A(4)` and `A(Int)` are told apart by the declaration, so a
-- default written at the wrong kind is the same mistake as an argument written at it.
data CountAsType(n: Int = Int) {value: Int}
data TypeAsCount(a = 4) {value: Int}

-- A defaulted parameter followed by one without a default. An application omits its *last*
-- arguments, so there is no spelling that reaches `b` while leaving `a` to its default.
data NotASuffix(a = Int, b) {left: a, right: b}

-- A class default on a head with two parameters. What a default answers is which type an
-- unconstrained variable settles to, and a class with two has no single variable to settle.
class Pairwise(a = Int, b):
  fn pair(x: a, y: b) -> Int

-- And one that names a type with no instance of its own class, which is a default nothing could
-- ever take.
class Unimplemented(a = Wrapper(Int)):
  fn use(x: a) -> Int
